Title

A Formal Theory of Runtime‑Enforceable AI Governance: Viability Control, Deterministic Policy Enforcement, and Verifiable Audit

Author

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

Autonomous AI systems challenge regulatory frameworks because their behaviour is non‑deterministic, path‑dependent, and not fully specifiable at design time. This dissertation develops a formal theory of runtime‑enforceable AI governance: the embedding of compliance enforcement directly into the inference path so that policy violation is operationally impossible for any observable action. The theory is built on four foundational pillars: (1) a viability‑kernel control framework for five‑axis governance drift with a two‑regime Lyapunov stability theorem; (2) a domain‑specific policy language (G‑Rego) with a verified translation pipeline and provable PAC bounds on policy extraction; (3) a distributed enforcement architecture whose sidecar placement is proven optimal via stochastic comparison under heavy‑tailed service, with a composition theorem for multi‑agent pipelines that explicitly accounts for the limits of syntactic contract refinement identified by recent impossibility results; and (4) a cryptographic Verifiable Governance Audit Trail using post‑quantum zk‑STARKs with bounded proof generation complexity. All inductive state invariants are machine‑verified in TLA⁺. A formal threat model classifies attacks by observability and capability expansion, and a hierarchy theorem establishes the architecture as optimal for its observation class. Every claim is supported by asymptotic lower bounds demonstrating fundamental tightness. The result is a verified blueprint for AI systems in which compliance is a machine‑speed, provable, and architecturally guaranteed property.

