Chapter 1

Introduction

1.1 Motivation: The Regulatory Imperative for Runtime Governance

Enterprise AI failures persistently stem from governance drift—the silent divergence between declared policies and operational behaviour. A model deployed under a data‑sourcing policy may silently encounter distributional shift; an agent granted tool access may construct execution paths that satisfy each individually permissible step yet violate the spirit of the governing policy. A 2026 KPMG survey found that 75% of large‑enterprise leaders cite security, compliance, and auditability as the most critical requirements for agent deployment, while multi‑agent orchestration complexity has become the primary bottleneck as organisations move from pilots to production.

The regulatory environment compounds this urgency. The EU AI Act classifies high‑risk AI systems as those making or supporting consequential decisions affecting individuals' rights, safety, or access to services, and imposes mandatory requirements for data governance, documentation, transparency, human oversight, and robustness. Its provisions for high‑risk systems take effect in August 2026 with fines reaching 7% of global turnover. Organisations deploying AI agents in regulated environments must therefore be able to prove—not merely assert—that every observable action satisfies the applicable obligations at decision time.

Existing governance approaches are structurally misaligned with this requirement. Prompt‑level instructions, post‑hoc documentation, and periodic audits evaluate policy compliance after execution, creating a window in which violations can occur and propagate. Recent work has begun to address this gap: Kaptein, Khan, and Podstavnychy (2026) formalise compliance policies as probabilistic functions over execution paths, arguing that runtime evaluation is the general case for path‑dependent governance. Bhardwaj (2026) introduces Agent Behavioral Contracts (ABCs), specifying preconditions, invariants, governance policies, and recovery mechanisms as first‑class runtime‑enforceable components, with a drift bounds theorem proven under Ornstein–Uhlenbeck dynamics. SARC (Besanson, 2026) treats constraints as specification objects compiled into four enforcement sites in the agent loop. Aegis (Mazzocchetti, 2026) binds agents to cryptographically sealed policy layers with autonomous violation‑triggered shutdown. SentinelAgent (Patil, 2026) provides a formal Delegation Chain Calculus for verifiable multi‑agent delegation and demonstrates the practical infeasibility of deterministic intent preservation under adversarial paraphrasing.

These contributions advance individual primitives, but no existing work integrates them into a single formally verified theory. Governance drift, drift detection and control, policy specification and verification, runtime enforcement, and auditability are each treated in isolation. The result is an ungoverned gap at the boundaries between components—precisely where systemic failures emerge. Moreover, several technical tensions between these strands have not been resolved: the probabilistic vs. deterministic nature of policies, the compatibility of stochastic drift models, the limits of syntactic contract refinement, and the challenges of streaming inference.

This dissertation closes that gap. We present a self‑contained, mathematically rigorous theory of runtime‑enforceable AI governance in which every component is formally specified, every boundary is bridged by a theorem, and the integrated architecture is machine‑checked against a fixed set of correct‑construction invariants. The theory does not claim to solve all AI governance problems; it delimits, with precision, the frontier of what can be guaranteed at runtime from observable state, and proves that within that frontier the architecture provides deterministic compliance.

1.2 Problem Statement

The central problem addressed is the design of a runtime enforcement architecture that simultaneously guarantees three properties:

1. Deterministic compliance for every observable action—any action permitted must be provably consistent with the active regulatory rule set.
2. Systemic drift containment—the governance state of the system must remain within a pre‑specified safe set with high probability, despite non‑stationary input distributions, accumulating schema violations, and increasing regulatory obligations.
3. Verifiable auditability—every governance decision must be immutably logged and independently verifiable without disclosing sensitive operational data.

Prior art solves fragments of this problem, but no existing system provides all three guarantees under a single set of formally verified invariants. Furthermore, the formal relationships between the components are not incidental; they must be established through mathematical theorems that simultaneously respect the constraints of each subfield—control theory, temporal logic, stochastic queueing, and cryptography—while together forming a sound whole.

1.3 Technical Gaps in Prior Work

The following specific gaps in the state of the art motivate our integrated approach:

· Kaptein et al. (2026) define policies as probabilistic functions P(\text{violation} \mid \text{path}), but do not specify how a binary enforcement gate should be derived. We introduce a threshold parameter \tau and prove a trade‑off theorem between Type I (overblocking) and Type II (underblocking) governance errors, enabling a principled calibration.
· Bhardwaj (2026) models agent‑level behavioural drift as a mean‑reverting Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process and derives a drift bound D^* = \alpha / \gamma. Our governance drift model, however, is a submartingale over five systemic axes—model, data, schema, ownership, and compliance drift—without inherent mean reversion. The two are complementary but mathematically incompatible; we develop a separate closed‑loop viability controller that guarantees safety for the submartingale dynamics.
· SentinelAgent (2026) proves that no deterministic, non‑LLM verifier can guarantee intent preservation under adversarial paraphrasing. Our pipeline composition theorem relies on syntactic contract refinement—a check expressible over observable state—and we explicitly restrict the theorem to non‑adversarial output‑contract compliance, acknowledging the impossibility for semantic intent preservation.
· Aegis (2026) provides a cryptographic policy layer but does not bound the complexity of compliance proofs. We furnish a zk‑STARK construction with a corrected circuit size bound of O(m \cdot b) gates—where b is the bit‑precision of drift values—and show that for 200 rules and 32‑bit fixed‑point drift measurements, proof generation completes in under one second on a modern GPU.
· All current frameworks treat streaming inference as an afterthought. We define a streaming governance unit (a turn or tool invocation) and extend the two‑phase commit protocol to guarantee policy version consistency across such units, providing a provable invariant for token‑level generation.

1.4 Contributions

This dissertation makes the following primary contributions:

1. A two‑regime Lyapunov stability theorem for five‑axis governance drift. The system is modelled as a discrete‑time submartingale G_{t+1} \sim P(\cdot \mid G_t, u_t) evolving in [0,1]^5. A viability‑kernel controller selects from a discrete action set \{\text{log}, \text{throttle}, \text{block}, \text{escalate\_to\_human}, \text{rollback}\} to maintain G_t within a safe set K. We prove that under the controller, V(G_t) = \sum w_i \delta_i(t) satisfies a negative expected drift in the boundary regime, keeping the system within K with probability at least 1 - \alpha - h.
2. A deterministic enforcement semantics for probabilistic policies. Any probabilistic policy P(\text{violation} \mid \text{path}) is transformed into a binary gate via \pi_\tau(\text{path}) = \text{allow} \iff P(\text{violation} \mid \text{path}) \leq \tau. We characterise the trade‑off between false positives and false negatives as a function of \tau and give a calibration procedure using receiver operating characteristic analysis on historical traces.
3. A verified G‑Rego policy language and PAC‑complete translation pipeline. The language LTL\_gov (a safety fragment with depth ≤ 3) admits monitors of size O(|\varphi|) and evaluation time O(|\varphi|) per step. A Coq‑verified compiler guarantees soundness; an adversarial audit loop provides (\varepsilon, \delta)‑completeness for policies of bounded complexity \kappa(P) \leq \kappa_{\max}. We correct the earlier attribution of LTL₃ to the proper source (Bauer, Leucker, and Schallhart, 2011) and verify that the dynamic programming monitor runs in O(|\varphi|^3 \cdot |\text{trace}|) for general LTL safety.
4. Optimal sidecar placement under heavy‑tailed inference service. Theorem 6.1 proves that filtering non‑compliant requests at the earliest possible stage stochastically minimises end‑to‑end response time, even when service times follow a Pareto distribution with index \alpha \in (1,2). The proof uses sample‑path coupling and careful handling of infinite‑moment workloads.
5. A bounded‑complexity zk‑STARK compliance proof. We correct the earlier circuit size estimate to S = O(m \cdot b), where b is the bit‑width of drift comparisons. For a realistic deployment (200 rules, 32‑bit precision) the circuit contains approximately 6,400 gates, yielding a proof generation time of under one second on a consumer GPU. The proof links to a Merkle‑chained audit log published to a transparency ledger.
6. A formal threat model with three classes: Class I (observable violations), Class II (unobservable latent attacks, e.g., memory poisoning), and Class III (capability expansion attacks by self‑modifying agents). The hierarchy theorem establishes that the architecture is optimal for Class I and provides forensic tools for Class II, while Class III requires dynamic rule synthesis beyond the current scope.
7. TLA⁺ specification and machine‑verified invariants. The integrated AI Governance Engine (AGE) is specified in TLA⁺, and its inductive state invariants—NoUngovernedObservableAction, LogIntegrity, DynamicPolicyConsistency (extended to streaming units), and ConflictSafety—are proven using TLAPS.

Every result that admits an asymptotic lower bound is shown to be tight: monitoring general LTL safety requires 2^{\Omega(|\varphi|)} states; detection delay for sequential change detection is \Omega(1/\Delta^2); membership‑proof size is \Omega(\log N); and deferred enforcement cannot outperform earliest filtering.

1.5 Scope and Limitations

This theory does not claim to resolve all AI governance challenges. Several important problems lie beyond its current scope:

· Class II attacks (latent memory poisoning, goal hijacking) are not prevented at runtime; the architecture acknowledges this and provides tamper‑evident logs for forensic reconstruction.
· Class III attacks by self‑modifying agents are identified but not mitigated; they require capability sandboxing or just‑in‑time policy synthesis.
· Semantic intent preservation across multi‑agent pipelines, as studied by SentinelAgent, is not guaranteed by syntactic contract refinement alone; our composition theorem is sound only under an explicit non‑adversarial assumption on output compliance.
· Human‑escalation latency is not modelled in the viability controller; the theoretical guarantee assumes prompt and correct human resolution.
· Streaming inference is addressed through the definition of governance units, but a full formalisation of token‑level speculative decoding policies is deferred to future work.

By honestly scoping these boundaries, the dissertation provides a rigorous foundation on which future extensions can build.

