The remainder of this dissertation is organised as follows. Chapter 2 recapitulates the necessary mathematical preliminaries. Chapter 3 develops the five‑axis governance drift model and the viability‑kernel controller. Chapter 4 presents the G‑Rego language, LTL\_gov fragment, and verified translation pipeline. Chapter 5 formalises deterministic enforcement for probabilistic policies and embeds agent behavioral contracts. Chapter 6 proves the optimal sidecar placement theorem and the pipeline composition result under non‑adversarial conditions. Chapter 7 details the sequential change detection and Granger‑causal lead‑lag prediction for drift axes. Chapter 8 constructs the VGAT with bounded‑complexity STARKs. Chapter 9 specifies the integrated AI Governance Engine in TLA⁺, defines the three‑class threat model, and proves the hierarchy theorem. Chapter 10 addresses multi‑jurisdictional conflict resolution. Chapter 11 reviews related work in depth, and Chapter 12 concludes with a discussion of practical feasibility and future directions.

Chapter 2

Mathematical Preliminaries

This chapter recalls the mathematical foundations on which the subsequent theory is built. The material is standard, but we fix notation and state the theorems in the precise form required later.

---

2.1 Viability Theory and Stochastic Dynamic Programming

Consider a discrete-time controlled dynamical system

G_{t+1} \sim P(\cdot \mid G_t, u_t), \qquad G_t \in \mathbb{R}^n,\; u_t \in \mathcal{U},

where \mathcal{U} is a finite set of actions. The system must respect a compact constraint set K \subset \mathbb{R}^n. A policy \pi : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathcal{U} is viable if, for every initial state G_0 \in K, the closed-loop process satisfies G_t \in K for all t \ge 0 almost surely.

Definition 2.1 (Stochastic Viability Kernel). For a given confidence level \alpha \in [0,1), the \alpha-viability kernel \operatorname{Viab}^\alpha(K) is the set of all states G \in K for which there exists a (possibly non-stationary) policy \pi such that

\Pr\!\bigl(\forall t \ge 0 : G_t \in K \mid G_0 = G,\; \text{policy } \pi\bigr) \;\ge\; 1-\alpha .

When \alpha = 0 we speak of the exact viability kernel; for \alpha > 0 it is the stochastic viability kernel with safety probability at least 1-\alpha.

The computation of \operatorname{Viab}^\alpha(K) in the discrete-action, discrete-time setting can be performed by value iteration on the auxiliary optimal control problem

V(G) = \min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \Bigl\{ \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \mathbf{1}_{G^+ \notin K} + \gamma \, V(G^+) \mid G, u \bigr] \Bigr\},

where \gamma \in (0,1] is a discount factor and \mathbf{1}_{G^+ \notin K} is the indicator of constraint violation. The viability kernel is the set of states for which the value function remains below 1 (for \gamma=1 and undiscounted total violation probability). In practice, for the five-dimensional governance state we discretise each axis into L levels, obtaining a finite Markov decision process (MDP) with |\mathcal{U}| = 5 actions and L^5 states. Standard dynamic programming yields an \varepsilon-approximation of the viability kernel in time polynomial in L^5 and \log(1/\varepsilon) [1]. Because L is chosen modestly (e.g., L=50 gives 3.125 \times 10^8 states, manageable with sparse representations), this approach is computationally feasible. Importantly, the discrete action set avoids the need for Lagrangian methods that assume continuous control; we directly solve the MDP.

---

2.2 Linear Temporal Logic and Runtime Monitoring

Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) extends propositional logic with temporal modalities: \Box (always), \Diamond (eventually), \bigcirc (next), and \mathcal{U} (until). Over finite traces, the three-valued semantics LTL_3 [2] assigns to each finite prefix a verdict from \{\top, \bot, ?\}, where ‘?’ indicates that the prefix has not yet definitively satisfied or violated the property.

Definition 2.2 (Safety Property). An LTL formula \varphi defines a safety property if for every infinite trace \sigma \not\models \varphi there exists a finite prefix \sigma[0..k] such that all infinite extensions of that prefix also violate \varphi. Safety properties are monitorable: a deterministic finite automaton can reject any violating prefix in finite time.

We will use the polynomial-time monitoring algorithm of Bauer, Leucker, and Schallhart [2] that evaluates an arbitrary LTL safety formula over a finite trace of length n in time O(|\varphi|^3 \cdot n). The algorithm constructs a Büchi automaton and dynamically tracks the possible states without building the full deterministic monitor.

For our performance-critical enforcement path we restrict attention to the fragment LTL_{\text{gov}} defined in Chapter 4, whose formulas admit monitors of size O(|\varphi|) and per-step evaluation time O(|\varphi|), offering a significant practical speed-up.

---

2.3 Sequential Change Detection

Let \{X_t\} be a sequence of independent observations. Before an unknown change-point \nu, the observations are drawn from distribution F_0; after \nu they are drawn from F_1. The CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) test of Page [3] accumulates the log-likelihood ratio

S_0 = 0,\qquad S_{t} = \max\!\bigl(0,\; S_{t-1} + \ell(X_t)\bigr),

where \ell(x) = \log \frac{f_1(x)}{f_0(x)}. An alarm is raised at the first time T_A = \inf\{t \ge 0 : S_t > h\}.

Theorem 2.1 (Lorden [4]). Under the minimax criterion, the CUSUM procedure minimises the worst-case expected detection delay among all procedures with a given false-alarm rate. The optimal detection delay scales as \Theta(1/\Delta^2) when the Kullback-Leibler divergence between F_0 and F_1 is of order \Delta^2.

This optimality is asymptotic; for specific pre-change distributions Hadjiliadis and Moustakides [5] proved exact finite-sample optimality.

---

2.4 Cryptographic Primitives and zk‑STARKs

A zk‑STARK (Zero-Knowledge Scalable Transparent Argument of Knowledge) is a non-interactive proof system for NP statements, introduced by Ben‑Sasson et al. [6]. Unlike pairing-based SNARKs, STARKs require no trusted setup and rely only on collision-resistant hash functions, making them post‑quantum secure.

Definition 2.3 (Arithmetic circuit satisfaction). For an arithmetic circuit C over a finite field \mathbb{F}, the statement “\exists w : C(x,w)=0” is an NP relation. A STARK prover generates a proof \pi that convinces a verifier of the statement’s truth, with the following properties:

· Completeness: If the prover knows a valid witness w, the verifier always accepts.
· Knowledge soundness: Any polynomial-time prover that does not know w can cause acceptance only with probability negligible in the security parameter.
· Zero-knowledge: The verifier learns nothing beyond the truth of the statement.

The proving time scales quasilinearly in the circuit size |C|: O(|C| \log |C|) field operations. The proof size is polylogarithmic: O(\log^2 |C|) elements.

For our application, the circuit encodes the evaluation of a G‑Rego policy over a governance state. As we show in Chapter 8, each atomic comparison \delta_i < \theta requires only O(b) gates for a b-bit fixed-point number, so the total circuit size for a rule set with m predicates is S = O(m \cdot b). For typical parameters (m=200, b=32) the circuit contains roughly 6.4 \times 10^3 gates, making proof generation feasible in under one second on a consumer GPU. This corrects an earlier overestimate that used k^2 factor from bilinear forms; linear inequality checks do not incur quadratic cost.

---

2.5 Queueing Theory and Stochastic Comparison

A G/G/1 queue has general inter-arrival and service-time distributions. When service times are heavy-tailed—for instance, Pareto with shape parameter \alpha \in (1,2)—the mean service time is finite but the variance is infinite, and standard Kingman bounds are vacuous. Li [7] develops a framework for stochastic comparison of queueing systems, where one says that random variable X is stochastically smaller than Y, written X \le_{\text{st}} Y, if F_X(t) \ge F_Y(t) for all t.

Theorem 2.2 (Couponing proof technique). To show W_A \le_{\text{st}} W_B for waiting times in two queueing systems, it suffices to construct a common probability space (coupling) on which every sample path satisfies W_A(\omega) \le W_B(\omega). Such a coupling directly implies stochastic dominance.

We will employ this technique in Chapter 6 to prove that filtering non-compliant requests at the earliest possible stage minimises response time. The proof must handle heavy-tailed service times; we achieve this by coupling the arrival epochs and service demands identically and arguing that discarding jobs early can only reduce the remaining workload in a work-conserving system. The argument is valid under the excess-work formulation even when higher moments diverge, because it compares finite-horizon workloads that are almost surely finite.

---

2.6 Granger Causality in Vector Autoregressions

For a vector time series \Delta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^p, a VAR(q) model postulates

\Delta(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{q} \Phi_k \Delta(t-k) + \varepsilon_t,\qquad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma).

A time series j is said to Granger-cause series i if some coefficient \Phi_k^{ij} \neq 0 for a lag k. Hecq, Margaritella, and Smeekes [8] provide a post-double-selection procedure that gives valid asymptotic inference on Granger causality even when the set of relevant lags is unknown a priori. We apply their method to the differenced governance drift series, which are approximately stationary under the closed-loop controller (Chapter 3). Detecting lead–lag causality allows the controller to pre-emptively tighten thresholds for axes that are causally downstream of a detected drift, improving containment speed.

