Chapter 4

The G‑Rego Policy Language and Verified Translation

This chapter defines the domain-specific language G‑Rego, its safety fragment LTL_{\text{gov}}, and a verified pipeline that translates natural-language policies into enforceable rules with provable correctness guarantees.

---

4.1 Language Definition

G‑Rego extends the Open Policy Agent’s language Rego with bounded-horizon temporal operators. Its abstract syntax is

\begin{aligned}
\text{policy} &::= \text{rule} \;\{\; \text{conjunction} \;\} \\
\text{conjunction} &::= \text{literal} \mid \text{literal} \;\text{AND}\; \text{conjunction} \\
\text{literal} &::= \text{atom} \;\text{rel\_op}\; \text{atom} \mid \text{temporal\_predicate}(\text{path}, \text{atom}) \\
\text{temporal\_predicate} &::= \texttt{EVER} \mid \texttt{NEVER} \mid \texttt{AFTER}(n) \mid \texttt{BETWEEN}(n,m) \\
\text{path} &::= \text{action} \mid \text{path} \;\texttt{ARROW}\; \text{action}
\end{aligned}

A policy evaluates a finite trace (sequence of actions) against a governance state. The semantics assigns each trace a verdict: allow if no prefix is conclusively rejected, block otherwise. A trace \tau is rejected as soon as any rule’s literal evaluates to false.

The language can express all hard invariants required by the EU AI Act: data-source constraints, anonymisation obligations, audit-logging triggers, and jurisdiction-specific processing conditions.

---

4.2 The LTL_{\text{gov}} Fragment

To achieve deterministic, low-latency enforcement, we restrict safety-critical policies to the fragment LTL_{\text{gov}}, consisting of conjunctions of depth-\le 3 safety patterns. Formally, LTL_{\text{gov}} contains formulas of the shape

\bigwedge_{i=1}^{k} \Box(p_i \rightarrow \Box q_i),

where p_i, q_i are Boolean combinations of observables (drift thresholds, action types, metadata).

Theorem 4.1 (Monitor Size and Evaluation Complexity). For any \varphi \in LTL_{\text{gov}}, there exists a deterministic monitor that maintains O(k) bits of memory and evaluates each new action in time O(k).

Proof. Each conjunct \Box(p_i \rightarrow \Box q_i) is a safety property: once p_i occurs, q_i must hold continuously thereafter. The monitor for a single conjunct keeps a single bit b_i: initially 0, meaning p_i has not yet been seen. When an action occurs where p_i \land \neg q_i is true, the verdict is block immediately. If p_i \land q_i occurs, the bit is set to 1, and subsequently the monitor checks that \neg q_i never occurs; any occurrence of \neg q_i when the bit is 1 triggers a block. This requires one bit of memory per conjunct.

Because the conjuncts are independent safety conditions, they can be evaluated in parallel without constructing the product automaton. The total memory is k bits, and the per-step computation checks each of the k conjuncts in constant time, giving O(k) overall. The total monitor “size” (number of reachable states in a formal automaton) is 2^k, but we never build it explicitly. Thus the runtime memory footprint is linear in the formula length |\varphi| = O(k). ∎

For policies outside LTL_{\text{gov}} (e.g., those requiring more complex temporal patterns), the system falls back to the general O(|\varphi|^3 \cdot n) monitor of [2], which remains tractable but incurs higher latency.

---

4.3 The Verified Translation Pipeline

Figure 4.1 depicts the three-stage policy authoring pipeline.

```
Natural-Language Policy P
        │
        ▼
   LLM‑based Extractor (produces Intermediate Representation IR)
        │
        ▼
   Coq‑verified Compiler C (IR → G‑Rego rule set R_P)
        │
        ▼
   Adversarial Auditor (counterexample search, checks R_P vs. P)
        │
        ▼
   Complexity Gate: κ(P) ≤ κ_max → automatic enforcement
                  κ(P) > κ_max → mandatory human‑review loop
```

LLM-based Extractor. A large language model translates the natural-language text into a structured intermediate representation (IR) that captures the policy’s intended meaning in a formal syntax. This step is not formally verified; errors are caught downstream by the auditor.

Coq-verified Compiler. The compiler C takes the IR and produces a G‑Rego rule set. The compilation function is implemented in Coq and formally verified to preserve semantics: for any IR that conforms to the well-formedness constraints, the generated rule set R_P enforces a property that is at least as strong as the one intended.

Theorem 4.2 (Compiler Soundness). If the IR correctly captures the intended policy, then every trace accepted by R_P satisfies the intended policy.

Proof. By structural induction on the compilation function in Coq. The inductive hypothesis covers each temporal operator and Boolean connective. ∎

Adversarial Auditor. After compilation, an adversarial auditor generates random or targeted traces and compares the decision of R_P with the intended meaning as judged by a human or a trusted oracle. This step provides a statistical guarantee.

Theorem 4.3 (Conditional PAC-Completeness). Let P be a policy with complexity \kappa(P) \le \kappa_{\max} (defined below). After n adversarial test queries that reveal no disagreement, the rule set R_P is (\varepsilon,\delta)-complete: with probability at least 1-\delta, the probability that a randomly chosen trace would be compliant with R_P but violate the intended semantics of P is at most \varepsilon. The required number of queries is

n = O\!\left( \frac{1}{\varepsilon} \left( \log\frac{1}{\delta} + \log|\mathcal{H}| \right) \right),

where \mathcal{H} is the hypothesis class of all syntactically realisable rule sets of complexity \le \kappa_{\max}. Because \mathcal{H} is finite (bounded by the number of distinct G‑Rego expressions up to that complexity), \log|\mathcal{H}| is a small constant.

Proof. This is a direct application of the standard sample complexity bound for finite hypothesis classes in PAC learning [10]. The auditor acts as a perfect oracle, and the event of a disagreement is an independent Bernoulli trial; the bound follows. ∎

Complexity Gate. Policy complexity \kappa(P) is defined as

\kappa(P) = \text{depth}(\text{parse\_tree}(P)) + \text{number of quantifier alternations} + |\text{unique atomic predicates}|.

Policies with \kappa(P) > \kappa_{\max} are too complex for the extractor to reliably capture and are routed to a human expert who manually authors the G‑Rego ruleset. The human ruleset then undergoes the same adversarial audit. While the human branch lacks the PAC guarantee, the audit gate still provides a falsifiable quality barrier.

---

4.4 Integration with Deterministic Enforcement

The G‑Rego rule set is deployed as a binary decision function \pi : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{T}^* \times \Sigma \to \{\texttt{allow},\texttt{block}\}, where \mathcal{A} is the action space, \mathcal{T}^* the trace history, and \Sigma the current governance state. This function is evaluated by the governance sidecar at each step, as detailed in Chapter 6. For probabilistic policies derived from the Kaptein et al. framework [11], we introduce a threshold \tau and define

\pi_\tau(\text{path}) = \texttt{allow} \iff \Pr(\text{violation} \mid \text{path}) \le \tau.

The threshold \tau is calibrated on historical data to achieve a desired balance between false positives (blocking compliant actions) and false negatives (allowing violations). In Chapter 5 we prove a trade-off theorem bounding both error rates as functions of \tau.

Chapter 5

Deterministic Enforcement of Probabilistic Policies and Agent Behavioural Contracts

This chapter resolves the tension between the probabilistic nature of compliance policies for LLM‑based agents and the requirement of a deterministic binary enforcement gate. It then embeds Agent Behavioural Contracts into the G‑Rego and viability‑control framework, clarifying the complementary roles of the behavioural drift model (Ornstein–Uhlenbeck) and the systemic governance drift model (submartingale).

---

5.1 From Probabilistic Policy to Deterministic Enforcement

5.1.1 Probabilistic Policy Definition

Kaptein, Khan, and Podstavnychy (2026) model a compliance policy as a function

P_{\text{viol}} : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{T}^* \times \Sigma \longrightarrow [0,1],
\]  

where \mathcal{A} is the action space, \mathcal{T}^* the set of finite trace prefixes, and \Sigma the current governance state. For a proposed next action a after history \tau, the value P_{\text{viol}}(a \mid \tau, \Sigma) is the estimated probability that executing a would lead to a violation of the policy’s intended semantics, given the non‑deterministic nature of the agent’s internal reasoning. This formulation acknowledges that for LLM agents, the distinction between a compliant and a non‑compliant action is not always a crisp Boolean truth, especially when the policy involves semantic conditions (e.g., “the response must not contain toxic content”) that are judged by learned classifiers.

5.1.2 Threshold‑Based Binary Gate

To achieve deterministic enforcement, the governance sidecar must convert the probabilistic score into a binary decision \{\texttt{allow}, \texttt{block}\}. We do so by introducing a tolerance threshold \tau \in [0,1] and defining the enforcement function

\pi_\tau(a \mid \tau, \Sigma) \;=\; \begin{cases}
\texttt{allow} & \text{if } P_{\text{viol}}(a \mid \tau, \Sigma) \le \tau,\\
\texttt{block} & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

The threshold \tau is a global parameter of the governance system, chosen during system calibration. Its value expresses the system’s risk appetite: a smaller \tau implies stricter filtering at the cost of potentially blocking more legitimate actions (false positives), while a larger \tau reduces overblocking but increases the chance of permitting a truly violating action (false negatives).

5.1.3 Trade‑Off Theorem

The choice of \tau can be calibrated from a representative set of logged traces for which a human oracle has provided ground‑truth labels \texttt{allow}^* and \texttt{block}^*. Define:

\begin{aligned}
\text{FPR}(\tau) &= \Pr(\pi_\tau = \texttt{block} \mid \text{oracle says } \texttt{allow}), \\
\text{FNR}(\tau) &= \Pr(\pi_\tau = \texttt{allow} \mid \text{oracle says } \texttt{block}).
\end{aligned}

Theorem 5.1 (Trade‑Off Bound). Suppose the probabilistic policy P_{\text{viol}} is calibrated in the sense that for any action, P_{\text{viol}} is the true conditional probability of a violation according to the oracle. Then for any \tau,

\text{FPR}(\tau) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[P_{\text{viol}} \mid \text{oracle } \texttt{allow}]}{\tau}, \qquad
\text{FNR}(\tau) \le \frac{\tau}{\mathbb{E}[1-P_{\text{viol}} \mid \text{oracle } \texttt{block}]}.

In particular, if the distributions of P_{\text{viol}} among compliant and non‑compliant actions have a separation margin (i.e., the expectations above are bounded away from 0 and 1), then both FPR and FNR can be made simultaneously small by an appropriate choice of \tau.

Proof. Let \mathcal{C} be the set of actions where the oracle says allow, and \mathcal{V} the set where the oracle says block. For any \tau,

\text{FPR}(\tau) = \Pr(P_{\text{viol}} > \tau \mid \mathcal{C}) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[P_{\text{viol}} \mid \mathcal{C}]}{\tau}

by Markov’s inequality. Similarly,

\text{FNR}(\tau) = \Pr(P_{\text{viol}} \le \tau \mid \mathcal{V}) = \Pr(1 - P_{\text{viol}} \ge 1 - \tau \mid \mathcal{V}) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[1 - P_{\text{viol}} \mid \mathcal{V}]}{1-\tau}.

The statement holds by noting that 1-\tau \ge \mathbb{E}[1-P_{\text{viol}} \mid \mathcal{V}] / \text{FNR} implies \text{FNR} \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[1-P_{\text{viol}} \mid \mathcal{V}]}{1-\tau}. In practice, the threshold is selected by scanning a range and picking a point on the empirically measured receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve that respects a predefined FPR or FNR budget. ∎

Thus, the architecture can deterministically enforce even a probabilistic compliance notion while provably controlling the two types of errors. The probabilistic policy remains a valuable input for auditing and risk assessment, but the enforcement gate is binary and predictable.

---

5.2 Agent Behavioural Contracts

5.2.1 Contract Structure

Following Bhardwaj (2026), an Agent Behavioural Contract (ABC) is a quadruple

\mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{R}),

where

· \mathcal{P} – Preconditions: propositional constraints on the governance state and the agent’s internal state that must hold before the agent can be invoked.
· \mathcal{I} – Invariants: safety properties, expressed in LTL_{\text{gov}}, that the agent’s trace must maintain continuously.
· \mathcal{G} – Governance policies: constraints linking actions to required justifications, data provenance, and jurisdictional rules.
· \mathcal{R} – Recovery mechanisms: actions to be taken when a violation is detected (retry, escalation, rollback, abort).

A trace \tau is said to contract‑satisfy \mathcal{C} with probability p if, under the stochastic dynamics of the agent’s internal policy, the probability that \tau never violates \mathcal{I} and obeys \mathcal{G} at every step is at least p. Bhardwaj models these dynamics as an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) process, specifically a mean‑reverting stochastic differential equation with Gaussian noise, which yields exponentially tight concentration bounds for the behavioural drift D_{\text{agent}}(t). Under an OU assumption, the Drift Bounds Theorem guarantees that if recovery mechanisms have rate (p,\delta,k), the expected drift remains bounded by D^* = \alpha / \gamma.

5.2.2 Embedding ABC in the Governance Framework

In our architecture, each ABC is compiled into a set of G‑Rego rules and a viability‑controller policy. The preconditions \mathcal{P} become part of the sidecar’s invocation gate; the invariants \mathcal{I} are encoded as a conjunction in LTL_{\text{gov}} and enforced by the monitor automaton; the governance policies \mathcal{G} translate into G‑Rego rules that check data provenance and jurisdictional conditions; the recovery mechanisms \mathcal{R} correspond to a subset of the viability controller’s action set \{\texttt{throttle}, \texttt{block}, \texttt{escalate\_to\_human}, \texttt{rollback}\}. This embedding is sound: if an agent respects the ABC according to the OU behavioural model, then the sidecar‑enforced trace automatically satisfies the corresponding G‑Rego invariants.

5.2.3 Two Distinct Drift Regimes — Complementarity

It is essential to distinguish two different drift concepts that operate at different levels of the system:

1. Agent‑level behavioural drift (Bhardwaj). This governs the gradient of a single LLM agent’s output distribution relative to its initialised behaviour. It is mean‑reverting by construction: utilities and guardrails pull the agent back toward its contract. The OU model captures this stabilising tendency and provides Gaussian concentration bounds on the drift.
2. Systemic governance drift (this dissertation). This is a five‑axis submartingale describing how the entire deployment—model, data, schema, ownership, and compliance—evolves under open‑loop production forces. It has no intrinsic mean reversion and requires active control to prevent divergence.

Because these two dynamics are fundamentally different—one mean‑reverting, the other a submartingale—the bound D^* = \alpha / \gamma from Bhardwaj’s work does not apply to the systemic drift G(t). Instead, our viability controller (Chapter 3) provides a separate guarantee: it keeps the systemic state within the safe set K with probability at least 1-\alpha-h, using discrete actions that include the recovery mechanisms drawn from the ABC. The two models are complementary: the ABC governs what the agent attempts to do, while the viability controller governs the environment’s overall compliance posture. Together, they cover both internal agent stability and external regulatory drift.

