Chapter 6

Distributed Enforcement Architecture and Optimal Sidecar Placement

This chapter designs the runtime enforcement infrastructure. We model the inference pipeline as a directed acyclic graph of processing stages, each equipped with a governance sidecar. The principal result is a theorem proving that filtering non‑compliant requests at the earliest possible stage is stochastically optimal, even under heavy‑tailed service times. We then formulate a composition theorem for sequential multi‑agent pipelines, making explicit the limitations imposed by adversarial intent‑preservation impossibility.

---

6.1 Inference Pipeline Model

An inference pipeline is a DAG G = (V, E) where each node v is a processing stage: input validation, preprocessing, model inference, postprocessing, response generation. Each stage v has a service‑time distribution B_v and is served by a single server (the result generalises to multi‑server with a convex ordering argument). Inter‑arrival times at the first stage have distribution A.

A governance sidecar S_v is attached to every stage v. It evaluates a local rule set \mathcal{R}_v, which is a decomposition of the global policy such that

\bigwedge_{v \in V} \mathcal{R}_v \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathcal{R}_g .

If any sidecar rejects a request, the request is immediately discarded and a VGAT violation entry is logged. The sidecar overhead is a fixed deterministic constant d \ll \mathbb{E}[B_{\text{inference}}].

```
                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │  API Gateway │
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐   Sidecar S₀
                 │ Input Valid. │──────┤ identity, data schema
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼────────────────────────┐
                 │  Policy Decision Point (S₁)    │
                 │  ┌────────────────────────────┐│
                 │  │ ABC Evaluator + LTL_gov    ││
                 │  │ Pre‑compiled Decision Tree ││
                 │  └────────────────────────────┘│
                 └──────┬────────────────────────┘
                        │
        allowed ────────┼───────────── blocked → VGAT entry
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐   Sidecar S₂
                 │ Preprocess   │──────┤ drift thresholds
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐   Sidecar S₃
                 │ Model Inf.   │──────┤ model‑specific checks
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐   Sidecar S₄
                 │ Postprocess  │──────┤ output anonymisation
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐
                 │   Response   │
                 └──────────────┘
```

The earliest point where a full governance decision can be made is sidecar S_1, because it has access to the parsed request identity, the agent identity, and the full history of the session. Earlier sidecars (e.g., S_0) can perform lightweight syntactic checks but cannot evaluate complete policies.

---

6.2 Optimal Earliest Filtering

6.2.1 Queueing Model

We analyse the system as a network of G/G/1 queues. Stage v receives a fraction p_v of requests that have passed all upstream sidecars. The service times at the inference stage S_3 are modelled as heavy‑tailed: B_{\text{inference}} \sim \text{Pareto}(\alpha) with \alpha \in (1,2). This reflects the empirically observed long‑tailed distribution of LLM generation times. All other stages have finite‑variance service times.

Let T_{\text{total}}^{(k)} denote the end‑to‑end response time when non‑compliant requests are discarded at stage k (i.e., after being served by stages 1,\dots,k-1 but before entering stage k). The earliest enforceable filtering occurs at stage 1 (after the policy decision point).

6.2.2 Stochastic Ordering and Coupling

We aim to prove

T_{\text{total}}^{(\text{earliest})} \;\le_{\text{st}}\; T_{\text{total}}^{(k)} \qquad \text{for all } k > 1.

To compare the two systems we use sample‑path coupling. Construct a common probability space with identical sequences of inter‑arrival times and service requirements for all jobs. For each job, attach a “compliance flag” c \in \{0,1\} indicating whether it will eventually be rejected by the governance check. In system A (earliest filter), jobs with c=0 are discarded immediately after stage 1, before consuming any server time at stages 2,3,\dots. In system B (deferred filter to stage k), the same jobs are served by stages 1,\dots,k-1 and then discarded.

Theorem 6.1 (Optimal Earliest Filtering). Under the constructed coupling, for every sample path the departure time of the last job up to any finite horizon is not greater in system A than in system B. Consequently, the waiting times and sojourn times of all jobs satisfy W_A \le W_B almost surely, and therefore T_{\text{total}}^{(\text{earliest})} \le_{\text{st}} T_{\text{total}}^{(k)}.

Proof. We compare the total work in system at each point in time. Let W_A(t) and W_B(t) be the sum of remaining service times of all jobs present in the respective systems at time t. At t=0 they are equal. All jobs have the same arrival instants and identical service requirements across coupled systems. The only difference is that in system A, a job with c=0 never enters the server queue of stages 2,\dots,k-1, whereas in system B it does and consumes service there before being discarded.

Because service at each stage is work‑conserving and non‑preemptive, the workload in system B at any time is at least that in system A: the non‑compliant job’s workload at stages 2,\dots,k-1 is present in B but absent in A. Formally, one shows by induction on the sequence of events (arrivals, service completions, discards) that W_A(t) \le W_B(t) for all t. The induction step: when a new job arrives, both systems receive identical workload increments if c=1; if c=0, system A adds only stage‑1 work while system B adds stage‑1 through stage‑(k-1) work. Service completions remove equal amounts of work. Therefore W_A(t) \le W_B(t) holds invariantly. Since response time of the n-th job is the time its total service is completed, the earlier completion of workload in A implies that every job’s departure occurs no later than in B, almost surely. This yields the sample‑path dominance and hence stochastic ordering. The presence of heavy‑tailed service does not invalidate this argument because the comparison holds pathwise on the event that all service times are finite (which occurs with probability 1 for Pareto with \alpha>1, as the mean is finite). ∎

Corollary 6.1.1. Placing the governance decision point at the earliest stage where full policy evaluation is possible is optimal with respect to any cost function that is non‑decreasing in response time.

---

6.3 Multi‑Agent Pipeline Composition

When agents are chained sequentially—the output of agent A_1 becomes the input of agent A_2—the governance problem becomes compositional. We assume each agent is governed by its own contract \mathcal{C}_i = (\mathcal{P}_i, \mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{G}_i, \mathcal{R}_i) enforced by its local sidecar.

6.3.1 Composition Condition

The output of agent A_i is a trace (or produced artifact) that must satisfy the precondition \mathcal{P}_{i+1} of the next agent. Because preconditions are propositional assertions over observable governance state and artifact metadata, we can check them syntactically at the boundary between agents.

Definition 6.1 (Contract Refinement). Contract \mathcal{C}_i refines the precondition of \mathcal{C}_{i+1} if every trace that contract‑satisfies \mathcal{C}_i also satisfies \mathcal{P}_{i+1}. We write \mathcal{C}_i \sqsubseteq \mathcal{P}_{i+1}.

6.3.2 Composition Soundness

Theorem 6.2 (Pipeline Composition Soundness). Let A_1, \dots, A_m be a sequential pipeline where each agent’s contract \mathcal{C}_i is enforced by a per‑agent sidecar. Suppose that for all i < m, we have \mathcal{C}_i \sqsubseteq \mathcal{P}_{i+1}. Then the composite trace \tau = \tau_1 \circ \tau_2 \circ \dots \circ \tau_m satisfies the global invariant \bigwedge_{i=1}^{m} \mathcal{I}_i, provided that the output of each agent is non‑adversarially compliant with its contract.

Proof. By induction on i. Base case: \tau_1 satisfies \mathcal{I}_1 because agent A_1’s sidecar enforces it. For the induction step, assume \tau_1 \circ \dots \circ \tau_i satisfies \bigwedge_{j=1}^{i} \mathcal{I}_j. The precondition \mathcal{P}_{i+1} holds on the state after \tau_i by the refinement condition \mathcal{C}_i \sqsubseteq \mathcal{P}_{i+1}, hence the sidecar of A_{i+1} allows the agent to proceed and ensures that its operation satisfies \mathcal{I}_{i+1}. The composition is monotonic: once an invariant is established, no later agent can retroactively violate it because each operates on a distinct segment of the overall trace. ∎

6.3.3 Adversarial Limitation

The condition “non‑adversarially compliant” is critical. As demonstrated by SentinelAgent (Patil, 2026), no deterministic syntactic verifier can guarantee semantic intent preservation under adversarial paraphrasing. That is, a malicious or misaligned agent A_i could produce outputs that pass all observable checks of \mathcal{C}_i yet, due to subtle semantic shifts, violate the spirit of \mathcal{P}_{i+1}. Our refinement check \mathcal{C}_i \sqsubseteq \mathcal{P}_{i+1} is purely syntactic, evaluating predicates on observable state. Consequently, Theorem 6.2 is sound for non‑adversarial, specification‑conformant agents; in an adversarial setting, the refinement may fail to capture semantic violations. This limitation is inherent to any runtime enforcement architecture that uses only observable state and formal contracts—a boundary we explicitly acknowledge.

---

6.4 VGAT Integration and Streaming Units

In the sidecar pipeline, every governance decision (allow/block) and every contract boundary check generates a VGAT log entry, chained via cryptographic hashes (Chapter 8). For streaming inference, the governance unit is defined as a complete turn or tool invocation, rather than individual tokens. The two‑phase commit protocol for policy version changes operates on governance‑unit boundaries, ensuring that all tokens within a unit are evaluated under the same active policy version. The formal streaming invariant is developed in Chapter 9.

Chapter 7

Five‑Axis Drift Detection

The viability controller of Chapter 3 requires real‑time measurements of each governance drift component. This chapter develops the detection procedures for each axis: sequential change detection for model and data drift, exact detection for schema and compliance drift, and periodic monitoring for ownership drift. It also introduces a Granger‑causal lead‑lag analysis that exploits predictive relationships among the axes to tighten thresholds pre‑emptively.

---

7.1 Drift Measurement and CUSUM Detection

7.1.1 Model and Data Drift as Sequential Change‑Point Problems

Model drift \delta_M(t) and data drift \delta_D(t) are measured by comparing current observations with a baseline. For \delta_M, at each time step we obtain a sample of model outputs Y_t and compute an estimate of the 2‑Wasserstein distance W_2(\hat{P}_t, P_{\text{baseline}}); for \delta_D, we compute the Population Stability Index (PSI), i.e. twice the Jensen–Shannon divergence between the incoming data distribution \hat{Q}_t and the reference Q_0. Both quantities are non‑negative and equal zero when the distributions match exactly.

Because production systems may run safely for long periods, a sudden increase in either distance indicates a structural shift requiring intervention. We therefore treat the sequence of distance estimates \{X_t\} as a sequence of independent observations (or at least exchangeable within a regime) and deploy sequential change‑detection procedures that raise an alert as soon as the mean deviates significantly from the acceptable baseline.

The standard tool is the CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) test of Page, proven minimax‑optimal by Lorden. We define the log‑likelihood ratio \ell(x) = \log(f_1(x)/f_0(x)) where f_0 is the pre‑change density (distance centred at a low nominal value) and f_1 is the post‑change density (distance shifted upward by a detectable amount). The CUSUM statistic is

S_0 = 0,\qquad S_{t} = \max\!\bigl(0,\; S_{t-1} + \ell(X_t)\bigr),

and an alarm is raised at the first time T_A = \inf\{t : S_t > h\}. The threshold h is chosen to control the false‑alarm rate.

Theorem 7.1 (Detection Optimality). Under the assumption that X_t are independent and identically distributed before and after an unknown change point, the CUSUM detector with threshold h = O(\log(1/\alpha_{\mathrm{FA}})) satisfies

1. False‑alarm rate \le \alpha_{\mathrm{FA}} under the pre‑change measure.
2. Worst‑case expected detection delay \mathbb{E}[T_A - \nu \mid T_A \ge \nu] \le \frac{2h}{\Delta^2} + O(1), where \Delta^2 is proportional to the Kullback–Leibler divergence between f_0 and f_1.

Proof. The false‑alarm bound follows from Wald’s likelihood‑ratio identity and the fact that the CUSUM process restarts from zero upon each dropping to zero. The detection delay bound is Lorden’s theorem [4], which establishes that the CUSUM minimises the worst‑case expected delay among all tests with a fixed bound on the mean time between false alarms. The asymptotic constant \Delta^{-2} is optimal for the class of procedures with access to the likelihood ratio. For exponential families, Hadjiliadis and Moustakides [5] prove exact finite‑sample optimality. ∎

In practice, the densities f_0,f_1 are estimated from a calibration period, and \Delta^2 is set to the minimum shift in drift that is considered operationally relevant.

7.1.2 Schema and Compliance Drift: Exact Detection

Schema drift \delta_{\text{Sch}}(t) is the normalised graph edit distance between the current data schema and the approved schema. Because schema changes are discrete and immediately visible to the governance sidecar (every data structure has a declared schema), any deviation yields a non‑zero \delta_{\text{Sch}} instantly. Detection is therefore exact:

\text{If } \delta_{\text{Sch}}(t) > 0 \text{ then a schema violation has occurred at time } t.

No sequential test is required. The same holds for compliance drift \delta_C(t), measured as the Jaccard distance between the set of active regulatory obligations and the set of those demonstrably satisfied. Any new obligation that appears without a corresponding proof of satisfaction immediately increases \delta_C, and the sidecar detects it with zero delay and zero false‑alarm probability.

7.1.3 Ownership Drift: Periodic Spectral Monitoring

Ownership drift \delta_O(t) is the normalised Laplacian spectral distance between the current data‑access provenance graph and the approved topology. This graph changes only when new data accesses occur, which typically happens at a frequency much lower than individual inference requests. We recompute the spectral distance on a daily (or batch‑job) basis and test for large deviations using Chebyshev’s inequality:

\Pr\!\bigl(\delta_O(t) \ge \theta_O + \varepsilon \bigr) \le \frac{\operatorname{Var}(\delta_O)}{\varepsilon^2},

where the variance is estimated from a historical safe period. If the observed distance exceeds the threshold by more than a margin, an alert is raised. This provides a probabilistic but adequate detection for slowly moving ownership structures.

---

7.2 Granger‑Causal Lead‑Lag Prediction

Under the closed‑loop viability controller (Chapter 3), the raw drift series \delta_i(t) are non‑stationary but their first differences \Delta_i(t) = \delta_i(t) - \delta_i(t-1) are approximately stationary and mean‑reverting to zero in the safe interior. We therefore model the vector \Delta(t) = (\Delta_M, \Delta_D, \Delta_{\text{Sch}}, \Delta_O, \Delta_C) as a vector autoregression of order p:

\Delta(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{p} \Phi_k \Delta(t-k) + \varepsilon_t,\qquad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma).

The matrices \Phi_k capture the dynamic dependencies among axes. An axis j is said to Granger‑cause axis i if there exists a lag k such that the (i,j) entry of \Phi_k is non‑zero. This means that past values of \Delta_j improve the prediction of \Delta_i beyond the information contained in past \Delta_i alone.

We apply the post‑double‑selection procedure of Hecq, Margaritella, and Smeekes (2023) to estimate the VAR consistently and to test for Granger causality without pre‑specifying the set of relevant lags. For each directed pair (j \to i), we test the null hypothesis H_0: \Phi_1^{ij} = \dots = \Phi_p^{ij} = 0 using a Lagrange Multiplier (LM) statistic, which is asymptotically \chi^2(p) distributed under the null. The false discovery rate across the 20 possible directed pairs among the five axes is controlled using the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure.

Theorem 7.2 (Causal Lead‑Lag Exploitation). Suppose axis j Granger‑causes axis i at lag 1 with coefficient \beta \neq 0 (p < 0.05 after FDR correction). Upon the detection of an increment in \Delta_j at time t, the viability controller can pre‑emptively tighten the safety threshold for axis i, e.g., from \theta_i to (1-\gamma)\theta_i for a small \gamma. This reduces the probability that the CUSUM detector for axis i will exceed its alarm threshold due to the anticipated cascade, effectively reducing the downstream detection delay in proportion to |\beta|.

Proof rationale. The Granger causality implies that \mathbb{E}[\Delta_i(t+1) \mid \mathcal{F}_t] includes a term \beta \Delta_j(t). A proactive reduction of the threshold decreases the chance that the future realisation of \delta_i crosses \theta_i. The exact reduction in expected alarm frequency can be bounded using the concentration properties of the innovations, but the principal effect is operational: by acting on the lead signal, the controller shortens the reaction chain. ∎

The CUSUM alarms and the Granger‑causal predictions are fed into the viability controller as additional state components, effectively augmenting the drift vector with “early warning” bits. The controller can then select a more stringent action even before the downstream drift value becomes critical, realising a tighter containment.

