9.2 Threat Model and Attack Classification

We classify attacks by the information they exploit relative to the observable state \text{Obs}(S)—the set of all attributes accessible to the sidecar at decision time: model inputs and outputs, provenance metadata, drift detector readings, active rule‑set identifiers, and the finite prefix of past actions.

Definition 9.1 (Observable Action Set). E_{\text{obs}}(S) is the set of actions whose intended‑policy compliance is fully determined by logical predicates over \text{Obs}(S).

Class I – Observable Violations. Attacks that can be expressed as violations of the G‑Rego rule set using only \text{Obs}(S). Examples: unauthorised data access, schema breaches, output failing anonymisation.

Class II – Unobservable Latent Attacks. Attacks that exploit internal agent state outside \text{Obs}(S)—latent memory poisoning, goal hijacking, steganographic triggers—where a malicious input, dormant for arbitrarily long, later causes a policy‑violating action while every individual step passes observable checks. The SentinelAgent impossibility result [Patil, 2026] shows that deterministic intent preservation is infeasible under adversarial conditions; this class formalises the resulting gap.

Class III – Capability Expansion Attacks. Attacks by self‑modifying agents that create new action types not anticipated at design time. A self‑modifying agent may generate helper functions, modify its own prompts, or instantiate new tools, thereby expanding the set of actions E(S) beyond the original E_{\text{obs}}(S) for which rules were written. This is categorically different from memory poisoning: the attack does not hide latent state; rather, it changes the agent’s effectivity in real time. No fixed rule set can cover an unbounded capability set.

This three‑class taxonomy is exhaustive for the information observable by a runtime sidecar attached to a single inference stage. We will prove that the AGE architecture is optimal for Class I, provides forensic evidence for Class II, and explicitly acknowledges Class III as requiring dynamic rule synthesis.

---

9.3 TLA⁺ Global State Machine

The entire AGE is specified as a TLA⁺ state machine with the following variables:

· log : sequence of VGAT entries.
· activePolicy : the currently enforced G‑Rego rule set, identified by its cryptographic hash.
· inFlight : set of ongoing requests, each tagged with policyVersion and governanceUnitId.
· driftState : the current five‑dimensional governance state G(t).
· controllerAction : the current action recommended by the viability controller.
· streamingUnits : a map from governance unit identifier to a status (open, closed) and the policy version under which it began.

State transitions correspond to the events: ReceiveRequest, EvaluatePolicy, ExecuteAction, UpdateDrift, ControllerTick, LogDecision, PolicyVersionUpdate.

The inductively checkable invariants are proven in TLAPS (the TLA⁺ Proof System). We emphasise that only inductive state invariants are machine‑verified; trace properties such as global policy soundness are established by structural induction over traces in the accompanying mathematical chapters.

---

9.4 Key Inductive Invariants

Invariant 1: NoUngovernedObservableAction.

\forall a \in \text{actions} : a.\text{allowed} \;\Rightarrow\; \left(\exists r \in \text{activeRules} : r.\text{eval}(a.\text{trace}) = \text{pass}\right).

Every allowed observable action has been explicitly evaluated and approved by an active governance rule.

Proof sketch. Induction on the action lifecycle. Initially, no actions are allowed. When a request arrives, the EvaluatePolicy transition sets a flag allowed only if the G‑Rego evaluator returns pass; the flag is cleared after execution. No transition sets allowed without evaluation. ∎

Invariant 2: LogIntegrity.

\forall i \in \text{Log} \setminus \{0\} : e_i.\text{prev\_hash} = H(e_{i-1}).

Every log entry correctly chains to its predecessor via a cryptographic hash, making the log tamper‑evident.

Proof sketch. The LogDecision action computes the hash of the previous entry and assigns it to the new entry’s prevHash. The only transition that appends to log does so with this property, and no transition modifies existing entries. ∎

Invariant 3: DynamicPolicyConsistency (for streaming).

\forall req \in \text{inFlight} : req.\text{policyVersion} = \text{activePolicy} \;\lor\; \text{req is closed within its current governance unit}.

In non‑streaming request‑response mode, this invariant collapses to the simpler statement that all in‑flight requests carry the currently active policy version. For streaming, we define a governance unit—a complete turn, tool invocation, or a bounded token window—that serves as the atomic boundary for policy changes. A governance unit is opened with the active policy version; the two‑phase commit protocol for policy updates waits until all open units under the old version have closed before activating the new policy. During the transition, requests of the new type are queued until the old units finish. The invariant guarantees that no unit spans more than one policy version.

Invariant 4: ConflictSafety (detailed in Chapter 10).

When multiple regulatory frameworks apply simultaneously, the conflict resolver permits an action only if there exists a rule that logically subsumes every applicable regulation and itself permits the action. In the case of incomparable regulations, the default is block.

Invariant 5: ViabilityBoundary.

The drift state driftState stays within the safe set K = \{G : \delta_i \le \theta_i\} as long as the viability controller is active and no Class II/III attack bypasses the sidecar’s observability.

This invariant is maintained by the controller’s actions; its inductive nature is verified by checking that each ControllerTick transition respects the Bellman optimality from Chapter 3, which we encode as a set of pre‑computed transition tables.

---

9.5 Streaming Governance Unit and Version Consistency

Modern LLM serving uses streaming token generation, which lacks a clean request/response boundary. We define a governance unit (GU) as a semantically complete segment: a user turn, a tool call with its result, or a token window of configurable length. The TLA⁺ state includes a map streamingUnits from guId to {open, closed} and the policyVersion at which the GU began.

When a policy change is requested, the orchestrator executes a two‑phase commit:

1. Phase 1 (Intent). Announce the new policy version; no new GUs may be started under the old version.
2. Phase 2 (Commit). Wait until all GUs that began under the old version have closed (i.e., their final token has been produced and evaluated). Then atomically switch activePolicy and start serving GUs under the new version.

During Phase 1, requests arriving under the old version that are mid‑GU continue; new GUs that would have started under the old version are queued. This protocol guarantees that the invariant req.policyVersion = activePolicy holds for each governance unit boundary. Within a GU, tokens are governed by the policy version at unit start; the exposure window is bounded by the maximum length of a GU. The formal proof that this protocol preserves all invariants is conducted in TLAPS by extending the state machine with a PolicyUpdate transition that triggers the two‑phase logic.

