9.6 Coterminous Governance and Hierarchy Theorem

Theorem 9.1 (Governance Hierarchy). For any runtime enforcement mechanism that blocks actions based solely on \text{Obs}(S), there exists a Class II attack that is undetectable at decision time.

Proof sketch. Construct an agent with a latent memory cell. At time 0, an adversary injects a value v that is not observable by the sidecar. The agent’s policy is: act normally until an observable trigger condition appears, at which point consult the latent memory. If v is a specific pattern, perform an action that violates the intended policy but satisfies all observable predicates at that moment. No observation of \text{Obs}(S) before the trigger can distinguish the malicious agent from a benign one, because v is unobserved. ∎

Theorem 9.2 (Coterminous Governance). Let E_{\text{obs}}(S) be the set of actions whose intended‑policy compliance is fully determined by predicates over \text{Obs}(S). The AGE architecture guarantees:

1. Every action in E_{\text{obs}}(S) is evaluated by the G‑Rego automaton and blocked if the rule set rejects it.
2. For any intended policy P with \kappa(P) \le \kappa_{\max}, the PAC‑completeness (Theorem 4.3) ensures with probability \ge 1-\delta that the enforced rule set blocks every action that would semantically violate P.
3. For Class II attacks, the AGE produces a tamper‑evident audit trail (VGAT) enabling post‑hoc forensic reconstruction, but does not claim runtime prevention.
4. For Class III attacks, the architecture logs the appearance of new action types (e.g., new tool signatures) and alerts, but cannot evaluate them against a fixed rule set; dynamic policy synthesis is required.

The governance boundary of the AGE is exactly E_{\text{obs}}(S)—it is optimal for its observation class, and the bounds are tight.

