Chapter 10

Multi‑Jurisdictional Conflict Resolution

When an AI system spans multiple jurisdictions, it may simultaneously be subject to the EU AI Act, US state‑level AI laws, sectoral regulations (HIPAA, PCI‑DSS), and organisational policies. These instruments are not designed for mutual consistency and may impose incomparable demands. This chapter formalises a conflict resolver that guarantees safety in the face of regulatory overlap, and proves that its block‑by‑default behaviour on incomparable rules is the unique safe default.

---

10.1 The Conflict Problem Formalised

Let \mathbb{R} = \{R_1,\dots,R_k\} be a finite collection of applicable G‑Rego rule sets, each expressed in LTL_{\text{gov}} or its Boolean closure. Each R_i defines a set of permissible traces \mathcal{L}(R_i). The system must decide, for each proposed action after history \tau, whether to allow or block.

A naive approach—permitting an action if any R_i allows it—can violate strict regulations: the most permissive rule would dominate, potentially violating others. Conversely, requiring that all rules allow it may be too strict if the rules are contradictory. The resolver must therefore examine the logical relationships among the rules.

10.2 Logical Implication and Meet‑Semilattice

Define a partial order on rule sets by logical implication:

R_i \preceq R_j \quad \text{iff} \quad \mathcal{L}(R_i) \subseteq \mathcal{L}(R_j),

i.e., R_j is more permissive than R_i. If R_i \preceq R_j for all j, then R_i is the strictest rule set. Under certain conditions, the set of applicable regulations forms a meet‑semilattice: for any R_i, R_j, there exists a greatest lower bound R_i \wedge R_j whose permitted traces are exactly those permitted by both. The meet operation corresponds to the conjunction of the individual rule sets.

Lemma 10.1. If all rule sets are in LTL_{\text{gov}} (conjunctions of depth‑≤3 safety patterns), then the meet R_i \wedge R_j is also in LTL_{\text{gov}} and can be computed by taking the union of the conjuncts.

Proof. Conjoining two conjunctions yields a larger conjunction; the depth remains ≤3 because each conjunct is of the form \Box(p \rightarrow \Box q), and we merely add more such conjuncts. ∎

---

10.3 Resolver Semantics

The conflict resolver operates on the active set \mathbb{R} and a proposed action a with trace \tau.

Algorithm (Conceptual, implemented by the sidecar as a decision tree):

1. Identify the subset \mathbb{R}_{\text{permit}} \subseteq \mathbb{R} of rule sets that would allow a after \tau.
2. If \mathbb{R}_{\text{permit}} = \emptyset, output block.
3. If there exists a rule set R^* \in \mathbb{R}_{\text{permit}} such that R^* \preceq R for all R \in \mathbb{R} (i.e., R^* is the strictest rule overall), and R^* permits a, then output allow.
4. If no such R^* exists—meaning the permissive rules are incomparable—output block.

Step 3 formalises the idea that if a single rule that logically implies all other regulations permits the action, then no regulation is violated. Step 4 is the safe default: when regulations are incomparable, there is no guarantee that an action permitted by one does not violate another; blocking is the only safe choice.

Definition 10.1 (Conflict Safety). A resolver is conflict‑safe if, whenever it allows an action, the action is permitted by every applicable regulation.

Theorem 10.2 (ConflictSafety Guarantee). The resolver semantics above is conflict‑safe.

Proof. Suppose the resolver allows action a. Then it must be in case 3: there exists R^* \in \mathbb{R}_{\text{permit}} such that R^* \preceq R for all R \in \mathbb{R}. Since R^* \preceq R_j, every trace allowed by R^* is also allowed by R_j. Because a is allowed by R^*, it is also allowed by every R_j. Thus no regulation is violated. ∎

Theorem 10.3 (Uniqueness of Safe Default on Incomparability). If the applicable regulations are pairwise incomparable (i.e., for any i \neq j, neither R_i \preceq R_j nor R_j \preceq R_i), then any deterministic resolver that is conflict‑safe must block the action.

Proof. Let the resolver allow action a. For conflict safety, a must be allowed by every R_i. In particular, it must be allowed by each R_i. Now take the strictest rule R^* among those that permit a; but since all rules are incomparable, there is no single rule that subsumes all others. Thus there exists some R_k that does not subsume R^*. By incomparability, R^* does not imply R_k, meaning there exists a trace \tau' allowed by R^* but not by R_k. Because a is allowed by R^*, it could be such a trace. Hence permitting a would risk violating R_k, contradicting conflict safety. Therefore, the resolver cannot safely allow any action; the only safe choice is to block. ∎

In practice, a human‑in‑the‑loop is invoked when incomparable regulations block a high‑priority action; the human may issue a reasoned waiver, documented in the VGAT.

---

10.4 Architecture of the Conflict Resolver

The resolver sits at the policy decision point S_1 in the sidecar pipeline (Chapter 6). It receives the set of rule sets applicable to the current request, evaluated in parallel on the trace prefix, and produces a single allow/block verdict.

```
   Active rule sets (R1, R2, ..., Rk)
         │
         ▼
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │  Evaluate each R_i  │
   │  on (τ, a)         │
   └─────────┬───────────┘
             │
             ▼
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │  Identify R_permit  │
   │  and test existence │
   │  of strictest R*    │
   └─────────┬───────────┘
             │
        ┌────┴────┐
