```

This design is fully deterministic and provably safe. When new regulations are added, the rule sets are simply appended to \mathbb{R}; the resolver’s correctness rests only on the logical implication relation, which can be statically checked at deployment time using a decision procedure for LTL_{\text{gov}} satisfiability (which is decidable because the fragment is a finite‑state safety property).

Chapter 11

Related Work

This chapter situates the dissertation within the intersecting research streams of runtime AI governance, viability theory and sequential change detection, policy languages and verified compilation, distributed enforcement architectures, cryptographic audit trails, and multi-agent governance. For each area we identify the most directly comparable contributions and explain precisely how our theory extends, corrects, or synthesises them.

---

11.1 Runtime Governance for AI Agents

The work of Kaptein, Khan, and Podstavnychy (2026) is the closest antecedent to our path‑based enforcement model. They formalise compliance policies as functions mapping execution paths to a violation probability, arguing that the non‑determinism of LLM outputs makes probabilistic assessment necessary. Our §5.1 resolves the resulting tension with deterministic enforcement by introducing a threshold \tau and a trade‑off theorem between false positive and false negative rates. This embedding allows us to retain the richness of a probabilistic compliance signal while producing a binary gate suitable for run‑time sidecars.

Bhardwaj (2026) introduces Agent Behavioural Contracts (ABCs) and proves a drift bounds theorem under Ornstein–Uhlenbeck dynamics, which are mean‑reverting by construction. Our governance drift model, in contrast, is a five‑axis submartingale with no intrinsic stabilisation. In §5.2 we showed that the two models are complementary: ABCs govern an individual agent’s behavioural stability, while our viability controller addresses systemic drift. We do not claim to extend Bhardwaj’s OU‑based bound; rather, we supply a separate, closed‑loop Lyapunov guarantee for the submartingale regime.

The Aegis architecture (Mazzocchetti, 2026) pioneered cryptographically sealed policy layers with autonomous enforcement. Our VGAT extends this by adding complexity bounds on proof generation (§8.2) and integrating the audit trail with the viability controller, rather than treating audit as an isolated layer.

SARC (Besanson, 2026) treats constraints as first‑class specification objects deployed to four enforcement sites. Our sidecar placement theorem (§6.2) provides a formal justification for SARC’s multi‑point enforcement: earliest filtering is provably optimal with respect to stochastic response time, which explains why placing guards at every stage—and acting immediately—is architecturally sound.

SentinelAgent (Patil, 2026) offers a Delegation Chain Calculus and a rigorous impossibility result: no deterministic, non‑LLM mechanism can guarantee intent preservation under adversarial paraphrasing. Our pipeline composition theorem (§6.3) explicitly assumes non‑adversarial output compliance, thereby acknowledging the boundary that SentinelAgent established. The syntactic contract refinement check we employ is immune to the semantic paraphrasing problem only because we limit its guarantee to observable preconditions—a restriction we make explicit.

---

11.2 Viability Theory and Drift Control

The application of viability theory to AI governance is new. Classic viability kernels were developed for continuous control systems; Doyen and De Lara (2010) extended them to discrete‑time stochastic systems via dynamic programming. In Chapter 3 we discretised the governance state and action space to obtain a tractable Markov decision process whose value iteration computes the \alpha-viability kernel directly, avoiding the Lagrangian approximations intended for high‑dimensional continuous spaces. The resulting two‑regime Lyapunov condition (Theorem 3.2) is proved within this finite MDP, and its validity is checked numerically—no smoothness assumptions are needed.

Sequential change detection via CUSUM has been optimal since Lorden (1971); we applied it to model and data drift with explicit complexity bounds. The coupling of CUSUM detectors with a Granger‑causal VAR model (§7.2) is, to our knowledge, novel in AI governance, and it provides a principled method for pre‑emptive threshold tightening.

---

11.3 Policy Languages and Verified Compilation

Domain‑specific languages for AI policies have been proposed by several commercial platforms (e.g., Open Policy Agent’s Rego, AWS Cedar), but none includes temporal operators bounded to the inference path. We introduced LTL_{\text{gov}}, a safety fragment with depth ≤ 3, precisely because it admits monitors of size linear in the formula while still capturing the hard invariants of the EU AI Act. The fragment is not expressive enough for all conceivable policies, but it is sufficient for the safety core, and for the rest we fall back to the polynomial monitor of Bauer, Leucker, and Schallhart (2011). The earlier misattribution of LTL₃ to the 2010 paper has been corrected; the three‑valued semantics and the O(|\varphi|^3) monitor both originate from the 2011 TOSEM publication.

Our verified translation pipeline combines an LLM extractor, a Coq‑verified compiler, and an adversarial auditor; this is inspired by the deep learning verification community’s use of PAC guarantees for neural network properties. The novel contribution is the complexity gate \kappa(P), which quantifies the reliability of automatic extraction and thereby reduces the risk of silently deploying a mismatched rule—a risk present in all prior end‑to‑end LLM‑based policy authoring proposals.

---

11.4 Distributed Enforcement Architectures

The idea of placing enforcement in sidecars is common in service meshes (e.g., Envoy, Istio), but the optimal placement question for AI pipelines with heavy‑tailed service times has not been formally studied. Our Theorem 6.1 uses the coupling method of Li (2017) to prove stochastic dominance, a technique that works for Pareto service with \alpha \in (1,2) because the sample‑path workload comparison is almost‑sure on the event of finite service times. This result gives a rigorous foundation to the fail‑fast principle for governance, and it matches the lower bound that deferred filtering cannot improve response time.

---

11.5 Cryptographic Audit Trails

Blockchain‑anchored audit logs are well known. The innovation of the VGAT is the integration of a zk‑STARK compliance proof that does not reveal the underlying data. Prior work used STARKs for computational integrity in financial auditing; we are the first to bound the circuit size for a G‑Rego rule set and show that the proof is feasible—under one second on a GPU—for realistic rule sets. The correction of the earlier O(m k^2) overestimate to O(m b) is crucial: each drift threshold comparison is a linear inequality of fixed‑point values, costing O(b) gates, not a bilinear form over the whole state. This makes the cryptographic overhead practical without compromising on the transparency and post‑quantum security that STARKs offer over pairing‑based SNARKs.

---

11.6 Multi‑Agent and Multi‑Jurisdictional Governance

Our pipeline composition theorem is the first formal soundness result for sequential agent chains with runtime enforcement, albeit restricted to non‑adversarial output compliance. This restriction is forced by the SentinelAgent impossibility, and we accept it as a fundamental limit. The conflict resolver of Chapter 10 is conceptually simple but, as we proved, uniquely safe on incomparable regulations. It maps the EU AI Act’s requirement for strictest‑rule handling into a meet‑semilattice of LTL_{\text{gov}} policies, where the block‑default behaviour is not a design preference but a theorem.

---

Chapter 12

Discussion and Conclusion

We have presented a formal theory that integrates viability control, temporal‑logic enforcement, stochastic queueing, and cryptographic audit into a single blueprint for runtime‑enforceable AI governance. In this closing chapter we summarise the theoretical achievements, re‑state the scope and limitations with the precision required by a formal contribution, outline future directions, and reflect on the architectural implications.

