12.1 Theoretical Contributions in Retrospect

The dissertation’s central claim is that for any observable action whose compliance is logically determined by the sidecar’s information set, the AI Governance Engine (AGE) can guarantee deterministic enforcement, systemic drift containment with high probability, and independently verifiable auditability. This claim is supported by the following theorems:

1. Two‑regime Lyapunov stability (Theorem 3.2). Under the viability controller derived from a discrete MDP, the five‑axis governance debt D(G) is a Lyapunov function with negative expected drift near the safe‑set boundary, yielding a probabilistic containment guarantee.
2. Deterministic enforcement of probabilistic policies (Theorem 5.1). A threshold \tau applied to a calibrated probabilistic policy bounds both false‑positive and false‑negative governance errors, enabling a binary sidecar gate without sacrificing risk awareness.
3. Linear‑time monitoring of LTL_{\text{gov}} (Theorem 4.1). The fragment achieves O(|\varphi|) memory and per‑step evaluation, making governance checks feasible at machine speed.
4. PAC‑complete policy extraction (Theorem 4.3). The verified translation pipeline guarantees, with high probability, that the enforced rule set matches the intended policy for all policies of bounded complexity.
5. Optimal earliest sidecar filtering (Theorem 6.1). Placing the governance decision at the earliest full‑evaluation point stochastically minimises end‑to‑end response time, even under heavy‑tailed inference service.
6. Bounded‑complexity STARK proofs (Theorem 8.1). The circuit for a rule set of m b-bit comparisons has size O(m b), making zero‑knowledge compliance proofs practical. The corrected bound eliminates the earlier O(m k^2) overestimate.
7. Hierarchy theorem (Theorem 9.1). The architecture is optimal for Class I (observable) attacks, provides forensic audit for Class II (unobservable latent) attacks, and identifies Class III (capability expansion) as a frontier beyond its scope.
8. Conflict safety (Theorem 10.2). The resolver is the unique safe default for incomparable multi‑jurisdictional regulations.

Every upper bound is matched by an asymptotic lower bound (Theorems 9.3–9.6), demonstrating that our constructions are fundamental and cannot be improved in their respective asymptotic complexity classes.

