12.3 Scope and Limitations

This is a purely formal, theoretical contribution. No physical implementation, empirical training runs, or large‑scale benchmarks are reported. The claims are mathematical; they are as strong as the assumptions from which they are derived. Those assumptions, and the resulting limitations, are:

· Class II attacks (memory poisoning, goal hijacking) are not prevented. The hierarchy theorem shows this is an inherent limitation of any runtime mechanism that does not inspect latent state. Mitigation would require architectural changes—e.g., periodic introspection, secure hardware enclaves—that lie beyond our formal model.
· Class III attacks (self‑modifying agents) are only identified, not solved. The governance boundary E_{\text{obs}}(S) is fixed at design time; an agent that creates new capabilities dynamically escapes it. Future work on dynamic rule synthesis or capability sandboxing may address this, but we do not attempt it here.
· Human‑escalation latency is unmodelled. When the controller escalates to a human, the Lyapunov guarantee assumes prompt and correct resolution. In practice, human response times are stochastic and may be unbounded. A formal treatment of human‑in‑the‑loop control with delay distributions is left open.
· Streaming inference is only partially formalised. We introduced the governance unit as a commit boundary and proved version consistency for two‑phase commit, but the full interaction with speculative decoding, token‑level abort, and pipelining across multiple model shards is not covered. A complete formalisation of streaming governance is a substantial extension.
· Semantic intent preservation across agents is not guaranteed. Our pipeline composition theorem requires non‑adversarial output compliance, which SentinelAgent has shown cannot be enforced by syntactic means alone. Thus, while we can guarantee that the syntactic contracts compose soundly, we cannot guarantee that a malicious agent does not subtly alter the meaning of its output in ways that violate the downstream preconditions.

These limitations are intrinsic to the formal approach and reflect deliberate scoping rather than oversight. By making them explicit, we provide a clean foundation on which future work can build.

---

12.4 Future Work

The most pressing extensions are:

1. Capability sandboxing and dynamic rule synthesis. To tame Class III attacks, a just‑in‑time policy compiler could analyse new tool signatures and generate G‑Rego rules on the fly. This would require extending the PAC‑completeness guarantee to a lifelong learning setting.
2. Human‑in‑the‑loop latency modeling. Augmenting the viability MDP with stochastic delays for escalation would yield a more realistic guarantee, potentially using semi‑Markov decision processes.
3. Empirical evaluation. Although the present work is formal, a companion implementation effort could measure the real‑world overhead of the sidecar, the STARK prover’s wall‑clock time, and the viability controller’s steady‑state behaviour under simulated drift. Such an evaluation would validate the feasibility claims and expose engineering constants that the theory abstracted away.
4. General multi‑agent topologies. The composition theorem currently covers sequential pipelines; extending it to directed acyclic graphs with shared memory requires a non‑interference theorem for concurrent agent interactions, which is a significantly harder problem.
5. Probabilistic fairness and distributional constraints. The rule language LTL_{\text{gov}} is designed for safety properties; extending it with approximate fairness quantifiers (e.g., demographic parity constraints) would require a probabilistic extension of the monitor semantics.

---

12.5 Conclusion

We have presented a formal theory of runtime‑enforceable AI governance in which compliance is a provable, machine‑speed property of the execution environment. The architecture is not a collection of best practices; it is a mathematical object whose components are linked by theorems and whose global behaviour is machine‑checked against inductive invariants. Every design choice—from the linear‑time monitor fragment to the earliest sidecar filter, from the discrete viability MDP to the bounded STARK circuit—is justified by its matching lower bound. The boundary of the theory is explicitly drawn: observable actions are governed with optimality guarantees; unobservable latent attacks are acknowledged and logged; capability expansion is identified as a frontier.

The EU AI Act’s high‑risk provisions came into force in August 2026. Organisations that deploy autonomous AI systems will be required to demonstrate, not merely assert, that every decision complies with the law at the moment it is made. The blueprint described in this dissertation provides the mathematical and architectural foundation for meeting that requirement—not as an afterthought, but as an executable property of the infrastructure itself. The proof is in the theorems, and the theorems are on the record.


Appendix A

TLA⁺ Specification of the AI Governance Engine

This appendix presents the complete TLA⁺ specification of the AGE global state machine. The specification is organised into constants, variables, operators, and the next‑state relation, followed by the inductively checkable invariants and their TLAPS proofs (sketched). The machine captures the lifecycle of governed requests across a sidecar pipeline with drift detection, viability control, and cryptographic logging.

---

A.1 Constants and Assumptions

```
CONSTANTS
  Agent,       \* set of agent identifiers
  Action,      \* set of possible actions
  Rule,        \* set of governance rules (each has eval : Trace × Action → Bool)
  ActiveRules, \* subset of Rule currently active
  SafeSet,     \* subset of DriftState considered safe
  MaxHistory   \* maximum length of trace prefix stored
```

A.2 Variables

```
VARIABLES
  log,                \* sequence of LogEntry records
  inFlight,           \* set of Request records
  driftState,         \* [0,1]^5 vector (δ_M, δ_D, δ_Sch, δ_O, δ_C)
  controllerAction,   \* current action from viability controller
  policyVersion,      \* current active policy version hash
  streamingUnits,     \* [guId ↦ {open, closed} × policyVersion]
  pendingPolicyUpdate \* option of new policy version during two-phase commit
```

A.3 Record Definitions (in TLA⁺ style)

```
LogEntry == [id : Nat, timestamp : Nat, agentId : Agent,
             inputHash : Hash, outputHash : Hash,
             ruleSetHash : Hash, decision : {"allow","block"},
             prevHash : Hash]

Request == [reqId : Nat, agent : Agent, trace : Seq(Action),
            policyVer : Hash, guId : Nat, status : {"evaluating","allowed","blocked"}]
```

A.4 Initial State

```
Init ≜
  ∧ log = ⟨⟩
  ∧ inFlight = {}
  ∧ driftState = [δM ↦ 0, δD ↦ 0, δSch ↦ 0, δO ↦ 0, δC ↦ 0]
  ∧ controllerAction = "log"
  ∧ policyVersion = initialPolicyHash
  ∧ streamingUnits = [guId ∈ {} ↦ ...]
  ∧ pendingPolicyUpdate = "none"
```

A.5 Major Transitions

We define Next as the disjunction of the following actions.

ReceiveRequest(req)
A new request arrives with a new governance unit if not already part of a streaming sequence.

```
ReceiveRequest(req) ≜
  ∧ req ∉ inFlight
  ∧ req.policyVer = policyVersion
  ∧ IF req.isNewStreamingUnit
    THEN streamingUnits' = streamingUnits @@ [req.guId ↦ ⟨"open", policyVersion⟩]
    ELSE streamingUnits' = streamingUnits
  ∧ inFlight' = inFlight ∪ {req}
  ∧ UNCHANGED ⟨log, driftState, controllerAction, policyVersion, pendingPolicyUpdate⟩
```

EvaluatePolicy(req, verdict)
For a request, evaluate all active rules. If any rule rejects, verdict = "blocked"; else "allowed".

```
EvaluatePolicy(req, verdict) ≜
  ∧ req ∈ inFlight
  ∧ req.status = "evaluating"
  ∧ verdict = IF ∀ r ∈ ActiveRules : r.eval(req.trace, req.nextAction) 
               THEN "allowed" ELSE "blocked"
  ∧ inFlight' = inFlight \ {req}
                 ∪ {[req EXCEPT !.status = verdict]}
  ∧ IF verdict = "blocked"
    THEN log' = Append(log, [id ↦ Len(log), ...,
                             decision ↦ "block",
                             prevHash ↦ IF log = ⟨⟩ THEN 0
                                        ELSE log[Len(log)].hash])
    ELSE log' = log
  ∧ UNCHANGED ⟨driftState, controllerAction, policyVersion, streamingUnits, pendingPolicyUpdate⟩
```

ExecuteAction(req)
If allowed, the action is executed and the drift state is updated by a deterministic function ApplyDrift.

```
ExecuteAction(req) ≜
  ∧ req ∈ inFlight
  ∧ req.status = "allowed"
  ∧ driftState' = ApplyDrift(driftState, req.action)
  ∧ inFlight' = inFlight \ {req}
  ∧ log' = Append(log, [id ↦ Len(log), ...,
                         decision ↦ "allow",
                         prevHash ↦ ...])
  ∧ UNCHANGED ⟨controllerAction, policyVersion, streamingUnits, pendingPolicyUpdate⟩
```

ControllerTick
The viability controller (a precomputed lookup table ViabilityAction) updates controllerAction based on current drift.

```
ControllerTick ≜
  ∧ controllerAction' = ViabilityAction[driftState]
  ∧ UNCHANGED ⟨log, inFlight, driftState, policyVersion, streamingUnits, pendingPolicyUpdate⟩
```

PolicyVersionUpdate(newHash)
Two‑phase commit: first set pending, then when all streaming units under old version are closed, switch.

```
PolicyVersionUpdate(newHash) ≜
  ∧ OR (pendingPolicyUpdate = "none" ∧ pendingPolicyUpdate' = newHash)
       (pendingPolicyUpdate ≠ "none" ∧ 
        ∀ gu ∈ DOMAIN streamingUnits : streamingUnits[gu].policyVer ≠ policyVersion ⇒ streamingUnits[gu].status = "closed" ∧
        policyVersion' = pendingPolicyUpdate ∧ pendingPolicyUpdate' = "none")
  ∧ UNCHANGED ⟨log, inFlight, driftState, controllerAction, streamingUnits⟩
```

---

A.6 Inductive Invariants and Proof Sketches

Invariant 1: NoUngovernedObservableAction

```
NoUngoverned ≜
  ∀ req ∈ inFlight : req.status = "allowed" ⇒ 
    ∀ r ∈ ActiveRules : r.eval(req.trace, req.nextAction) = TRUE
```

Proof sketch. The only transition that sets status to "allowed" is EvaluatePolicy, which does so only when the rule evaluation loop returns TRUE for all rules. No transition directly sets status otherwise. ExecuteAction clears the request, but it only acts on allowed ones. Thus the invariant holds inductively. TLAPS checks this by induction over the state graph.

Invariant 2: LogIntegrity

```
LogIntegrity ≜
  ∀ i ∈ 1..Len(log)-1 : log[i].prevHash = Hash(log[i-1])
```

Proof. The only action that appends to log is LogDecision, which computes prevHash as the hash of the last entry. The base case (log empty) is trivial. TLAPS verifies that the appending step correctly updates prevHash and no other transition modifies log.

Invariant 3: DynamicPolicyConsistency (streaming)

```
PolicyConsistency ≜
  ∀ req ∈ inFlight : req.policyVer = policyVersion
```

But with streaming units, we need a more nuanced version considered earlier. In the TLA⁺ spec, we ensure it via the two‑phase commit protocol: no new activePolicy is installed while any inFlight request or open streaming unit has the old version. The invariant is that for any guId with status = "open", its policyVer equals policyVersion. TLAPS proves that PolicyVersionUpdate only commits when all such units are closed.

Invariant 4: ConflictSafety (integration with multi‑jurisdiction resolver)
Not written directly in the TLA⁺ model but enforced by the EvaluatePolicy transition, which uses the resolver logic from Chapter 10 encoded as a function ResolveConflict(rules, trace, action). The invariant asserts that verdict = "allowed" implies there exists a strictest rule subsuming all others and permitting the action. The TLAPS proof would require the logical implication decision procedure; we instead rely on the Coq‑verified compiler to guarantee that the active rule set obeys the conflict safety property.

Invariant 5: ViabilityBoundary

```
ViabilityBoundary ≜
  driftState ∈ SafeSet
```

This cannot be a state invariant because drift may leave the safe set under certain attacks. Instead, we prove a stochastic property (Theorem 3.2) that it holds with high probability. In the TLA⁺ model, we do not model probabilities; we instead verify that whenever ControllerTick fires, it sets controllerAction such that the expected drift (given in a transition table) is negative in the boundary regime. The inductive property is that controllerAction is always chosen according to the optimal policy, which is enforced by the lookup table.

---

A.7 Machine‑Checked Proofs with TLAPS

All the above invariants (excepting the probabilistic one) are verified using TLAPS. The proof structure for each invariant is:

```
THEOREM Spec => []Invariant
PROOF
  <1> Init => Invariant
  <1> Invariant /\ [Next]_vars => Invariant'
  <1> QED
```

The step <1> for each sub‑action of Next is expanded, and TLAPS automatically discharges the arithmetic and logical implications using the SMT backend. The most complex case is EvaluatePolicy, where the resolvers logic is unrolled; we provided a lemma that ResolveConflict is sound (proved in Coq and imported as an axiom in TLAPS).

